  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="1">Confirm PR #1045 is merged and master is merged into this branch; re-verify every anchor above (line numbers will have moved) and re-run the caller audit — every `ResolveForRecordAsync` caller and where its name comes from.</step>
    <step order="2">Extend the registry to answer "is this a real entity" from the metadata query it already runs, with the caching rules in the constraints.</step>
    <step order="3">In the resolver: normalize through `DocumentAssociationMap.ToLogicalName`; if the result is not a real entity, throw the typed refusal; otherwise continue unchanged. Log the refusal with the supplied name (sanitized) and the caller-visible code.</step>
    <step order="4">Tests: alias → same decision as its logical name (secure and non-secure); unknown name → typed refusal, never a decision; real non-securable name → unchanged; metadata failure → propagates; cached old-format value → not misread; OBO route with an alias → correct outcome, not the misleading 409. Seed each guard by reverting its line and confirm it fails.</step>
    <step order="5">Build and run BFF unit + integration suites and NetArchTest; publish-size measurement per CLAUDE.md §10.</step>
    <step order="6">Comment on #1038 with the resolver-level fix (and close the new tracking issue); update TASK-INDEX.md (`✅ [done]`) and the POML status; record deviations in projects/unified-access-control-r2/notes/.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>The caller audit finds a caller that legitimately passes a name that is not a Dataverse entity logical name and not an alias (e.g. an entity set name like "sprk_projects"): STOP and report it before deciding whether the resolver maps it or the caller changes — silently accepting a new spelling is how #1038 happened.</trigger>
    <trigger>PR #1045 is merged with `DocumentAssociationMap.ToLogicalName` renamed or with different semantics than described (e.g. it no longer maps "contact"): adapt only after confirming the alias set with word-add-in-r1; do not fork an alias table here.</trigger>
    <trigger>The HTTP status/code for the refusal collides with an existing client contract (the Office add-in or the OBO upload client already branch on 400/404/409 codes): surface the options rather than picking one silently. Recommendation: 400 with the new stable code (an entity name that is not an entity is a malformed request), distinct from 404 `container_record_not_found` and 409 `secure_record_container_missing`, which existing clients branch on — grep the Office add-in and OBO upload clients for those codes/statuses first and record what they do with a 400.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <justification>
    <existing>`SecurableEntityRegistry.QueryMetadataAsync` (`SecurableEntityRegistry.cs:114-173`) already fetches every entity's logical name and discards all but the securable ones; `DocumentAssociationMap.ToLogicalName` (PR #1045) is the alias table; `EntityAccessFilter.EntitySetByType` (`EntityAccessFilter.cs:108-147`) is the route-level alias table. Grep for another "entity exists" metadata helper in Infrastructure/Dataverse returns none besides `MetadataService`.</existing>
    <extension>Yes — extend the registry interface with one method and one retained set; no new service, endpoint or package. The only new surface is one cache-key constant and one problem code.</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>Any caller that passes an unmapped or misspelled entity name for a SECURE record gets a non-secure decision, and the content is written to a shared SPE container that cannot be un-shared (#1038 was exactly this, live on master for Office saves). The OBO record-keyed routes keep answering alias names with a false "No storage container is configured".</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

